    "knowledge_point": "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit",
    "problem": "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
    "worked_solution": "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}5{x}^{-2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[(-5{x}^{-1})]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =-5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{x}]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =-5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{a}-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =-5\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
